How to actually verify on the bench
This is the part I can't do from a photo — it's a 60-second physical test:

Push the dev branch (with the fix) onto the Pi and start the robot at rest.

Expected: Cox row populates with status OK, sX and sY both single-digit mm, no relocalize spam. If you still see relocalize, the fix is incomplete — message me with the new dashboard output.
Place a single object (chair leg, broom handle) about 500 mm directly in front of the robot. Boot. Look at tools/viewer.py:

The dense scatter of LIDAR points should form a small cluster at world coordinates (≈500, 0) — i.e., on the +x axis, in front of the robot icon.
At (0, −500) → offset is right (current -π/2).
At (0, +500) → offset should be +π/2.
At (−500, 0) → offset should be π.
At (+500, 0) → offset should be 0.
Manually push the robot forward ~10 cm (don't drive — just slide it).

KF X should increase. If Y changes instead, the offset is 90° off. If X decreases, offset is 180° off.
Rotate the robot CCW (from above) by ~90° in place.

KF theta should increase by roughly +π/2. If it decreases by the same amount, the CW/CCW negation is wrong (your specific A2M8 or its SDK is reporting CCW already — flip the sign of the angle term in both call sites; don't change the offset).
If 1 passes but 2–4 fail, your LIDAR is mechanically aligned differently than the codebase assumed — change just LIDAR_MOUNT_OFFSET_RAD in include/global.hpp and rebuild. Both call sites pick it up automatically.

Bottom line
The fix is necessary and correct as a structural change (map and scan now share a frame — Cox can converge). The numerical value -π/2 is plausible for an A2M8 with cable to the robot's left, which matches what I can glean from the photo, but I'd rather have you confirm with step 2 above than guess from the image.

